/*!
\page qtbridges-qml-layouts.html
\title Starter layouts for Qt Quick desktop applications
\brief Eight small QML skeletons to use as starting points for your first desktop UI.

Every Qt Bridges application uses Qt Quick for its user interface, no matter
which programming language provides the backend. If you are new to Qt Quick and
QML, deciding how to arrange the window is usually the first hurdle.

This page collects typical desktop application layouts. Each one is a small,
self-contained \c{Main.qml} that you can paste into a new project, run, and then
grow. They intentionally contain placeholders instead of real content and no
backend logic, so you can focus on the structure.

\grid
    \griditem {#1-single-page}{1. Single page} {
        One centered area of content, such as a form or a tool.
    }
    \griditem {#2-header-content-footer}{2. Header, content, footer} {
        Toolbar on top, status bar at the bottom.
    }
    \griditem {#3-sidebar-and-content}{3. Sidebar and content} {
        Resizable side column, like modern editors and file managers.
    }
    \griditem {#4-tabs}{4. Tabs} {
        Top-level sections switched with a tab bar.
    }
    \griditem {#5-master-detail}{5. Master-detail} {
        A list of items next to the details of the selected one.
    }
    \griditem {#6-drawer-navigation}{6. Drawer navigation} {
        Hidden menu with a stack of pages.
    }
    \griditem {#7-dashboard-grid}{7. Dashboard grid} {
        Cards arranged in a responsive grid.
    }
    \griditem {#8-three-pane-workspace}{8. Three-pane workspace} {
        Tools, canvas, and inspector, like a design or IDE tool.
    }
\endgrid

\section1 Before you start

All the examples use three imports, for example:

\code [qml]
import QtQuick
import QtQuick.Controls.Material
import QtQuick.Layouts
\endcode

\list
\li \l{https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtquick-index.html}{Qt Quick} provides the basic
    visual items, such as \c Item and \c Rectangle.
\li \l{https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtquickcontrols-index.html}{Qt Quick Controls}
    provides ready-made controls and window parts, such as \c ApplicationWindow,
    \c Button, \c ToolBar, and \c TabBar. The part after \c{QtQuick.Controls}
    selects a \e style, which decides how those controls look.

\li \l{https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtquicklayouts-index.html}{Qt Quick Layouts}
    provides \c RowLayout, \c ColumnLayout, \c GridLayout, \c FlexboxLayout,
    and \c StackLayout, which resize and position their children automatically.
\endlist

A few rules of thumb apply to all layouts below:

\list
\li Use \c ApplicationWindow as the root item. It gives you slots for
    \c header, \c footer, and \c menuBar.
\li Inside a layout, use \c{Layout.fillWidth} and \c{Layout.fillHeight} to
    share the available space, instead of setting positions by hand.
\li Use \c anchors.fill: parent only for a single child that should cover its
    parent.
\li Give windows a \c minimumWidth and \c minimumHeight so your layout never
    collapses.
\endlist

\note The colored \c Rectangle items are only placeholders. Replace them with
your own components as soon as you have them.

\note Each layout uses a different Qt Quick Controls style, so you can see some
of the options. The style is not tied to the layout: to use another one, change
the \c{import QtQuick.Controls.<Style>} line, for example to
\c{QtQuick.Controls.Fusion}. The \c Basic, \c Fusion, \c Material,
\c Universal, and \c FluentWinUI3 styles are available on all platforms. See
\l{https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtquickcontrols-styles.html}{Styling Qt Quick Controls}
for the full list.

\section1 1. Single page

\target 1-single-page

A single centered area of content. This is the best start for small tools,
settings dialogs, login screens, and forms.

\image layout-single-page.png

Shown with the Material style.

\details {Show the code}
\quotefile layouts/single-page.qml
\enddetails

\section1 2. Header, content, footer

\target 2-header-content-footer

A toolbar at the top, a status bar at the bottom, and the content in between.
This is the classic desktop application frame.

\image layout-header-content-footer.png

Shown with the Fusion style.

\details {Show the code}
\quotefile layouts/header-content-footer.qml
\enddetails

\section1 3. Sidebar and content

\target 3-sidebar-and-content

A side column for navigation or a file tree, and a main column for the content.
The \c SplitView lets users resize both columns by dragging the handle.

\image layout-sidebar-content.png

Shown with the Universal style.

\details {Show the code}
\quotefile layouts/sidebar-content.qml
\enddetails

\section1 4. Tabs

\target 4-tabs

Top-level sections selected with a \c TabBar. Use it when the sections are
equally important and the user switches between them often. A \c StackLayout
shows only the page that matches the current tab.

\image layout-tabs.png

Shown with the Fluent (\c FluentWinUI3) style.

\details {Show the code}
\quotefile layouts/tabs.qml
\enddetails

\note In a real application, move each page into its own \c{.qml} file, for
example \c{OverviewPage.qml}, and use it as \c{OverviewPage {}}.

\section1 5. Master-detail

\target 5-master-detail

A list of items on the left and the details of the selected item on the right.
This is the natural fit for data coming from a backend model, such as contacts,
messages, or products.

\image layout-master-detail.png

Shown with the Material style.

\details {Show the code}
\quotefile layouts/master-detail.qml
\enddetails

\section1 6. Drawer navigation

\target 6-drawer-navigation

A menu that slides in from the side, plus a \c StackView to move between pages
and come back. It works well when you have many destinations, or when the
window can be narrow.

\image layout-drawer-navigation.png

Shown with the Universal style.

\details {Show the code}
\quotefile layouts/drawer-navigation.qml
\enddetails

\section1 7. Dashboard grid

\target 7-dashboard-grid

Cards arranged in a grid that changes the number of columns with the window
width. Use it for overviews, galleries, and status pages.

\image layout-dashboard-grid.png

Shown with the Fluent (\c FluentWinUI3) style.

\details {Show the code}
\quotefile layouts/dashboard-grid.qml
\enddetails

\section1 8. Three-pane workspace

\target 8-three-pane-workspace

Tools on the left, a work area in the center, and an inspector on the right,
with a status bar underneath. This is the typical structure of editors, IDEs,
and design tools. It combines the ideas of the previous layouts.

\image layout-three-pane-workspace.png

Shown with the Fusion style.

\details {Show the code}
\quotefile layouts/three-pane-workspace.qml
\enddetails

\section1 Where to go next

\list
\li Move repeated parts into their own \c{.qml} files and reuse them as
    components.
\li Replace the placeholder models with a model provided by your backend. See
    the documentation of your language for how to expose data to QML.
\li Explore the \l{https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtquick-positioning-layouts.html}
    {Qt Quick layouts documentation} to learn about anchors, alignment, and
    size policies.
\li For complete applications, see the
    \l{https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtquick-codesamples.html}{Qt Quick examples}.
\endlist

*/
